  "ai_language_tip": "Pashto (ps) is the default in the south and east and widely used officially; Dari (fa) is the common business language in Kabul, Herat, Mazar-i-Sharif. Start politely and follow the language the other side answers in; English only at a few international hotels and NGOs-facing businesses.",
  "english_ok": "rarely — Limited to some hotels, larger hospitals and firms used to foreign clients in Kabul; most shops, clinics and drivers speak only Dari or Pashto.",
  "politeness": "Open with 'Salaam alaikum' and a short courtesy before business; respectful titles ('ښاغلی' / 'آقا', 'محترم'); calls between unrelated men and women are kept short and formal; close with thanks and a blessing.",
  "name_tip": "Many people use a given name plus a family/tribal name, and some use one name only; give the name as the booking holder wants it written and spell it slowly; Latin spellings of the same name vary.",
  "phone_tip": "Mobile 07X XXX XXXX (10 digits with 0, e.g. 079 12345678), international +93 7X; WhatsApp is the usual channel for photos, locations and confirmations.",
  "time_tip": "Spoken time is 12-hour with part of day (صبح morning, چاشت midday, عصر afternoon, شام evening); confirm morning/evening explicitly. Dates usually follow the solar Hijri calendar (months حمل…حوت); the lunar Hijri calendar is used for religious days.",
  "number_tip": "Phone numbers read digit by digit or in small groups; both Eastern Arabic-Persian digits (۰۱۲۳) and Western digits appear in writing.",
  "avoid_calling": "Avoid Friday (weekly rest, Friday prayer), prayer times around midday and sunset, and late evening; during Ramadan daytime hours shorten and evenings are busy.",
  "closed_days": "Friday is the weekly day off; government offices work Saturday–Wednesday about 8:30–15:30 and a short Thursday until 13:30; Eid al-Fitr and Eid al-Adha close most businesses for several days.",
  "booking_channels": "Mostly direct phone calls, WhatsApp and walk-in; online booking platforms are rare; hospitals and private clinics take phone or walk-in queue tokens.",
  "voice_menu": "Mobile operators (AWCC, Roshan, Etisalat/MTN successors, Salaam) use IVR; nearly all shops, clinics and small hotels answer directly, often on a personal mobile.",
  "callback_habits": "Businesses usually call or WhatsApp back the same day; reminders and SMS confirmations are uncommon outside larger hospitals and hotels.",
